<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernal Díaz del Castillo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1492–<a href="/years/1584">1584</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernal Díaz del Castillo was a Spanish conquistador who took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>'s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in the early sixteenth century. In old age he wrote a detailed eyewitness history of the conquest. His account describes the astonishing volume and variety of goods in the great market of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a>, an important source on the economy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernal-diaz-del-castillo.jpg" alt="Bernal Díaz del Castillo" width="163" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernal_D%C3%ADaz_del_Castillo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bernal Díaz was born around 1492 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-del-campo">Medina del Campo</a>, a prosperous commercial city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. His father, Francisco Díaz del Castillo, was a regidor, or city councillor, which gave the family some prominence. He later showed a knack for languages, learning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaqchikel-people">Cakchiquel</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>.</p><p>In 1514, aged about eighteen, he left home as a common foot soldier in the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedrarias-davila">Pedrarias Dávila</a>, the largest fleet yet sent to mainland America, with 19 vessels and 1,500 people. At Darien, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, famine and an epidemic killed more than half of the settlers. In 1517 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-hernandez-de-cordoba-founder-of-nicaragua">Francisco Hernández de Córdoba</a>'s expedition to Yucatán, and in 1518 he returned to the Yucatán coast under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-grijalva">Juan de Grijalva</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, Díaz enlisted in the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> and took part in the campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a>, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>. He later described many of the 119 battles in which he fought, ending with the defeat of the Aztecs in 1521. Cortés awarded him an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> in 1522, confirmed and added to in 1527 and 1528. He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> in 1541 and in 1551, during a trip to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, was appointed regidor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de los Caballeros de Guatemala, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigua-guatemala">Antigua Guatemala</a>.</p><p>Like many conquistadors, he was discontent that he had not won the wealth he hoped for and felt that the Spanish government had failed to acknowledge his efforts. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> at the age of 92, in early January 1584.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His Historia verdadera de la conquista de la Nueva España, finished in 1568, was begun almost thirty years after the events and expanded after he read the biography of Cortés by Francisco López de Gómara. The title answers Cortés's own letters to the king and the claims of Gómara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> and others who were not on the campaign. Díaz held that Cortés took most of the credit and minimised the sacrifices of the other Spaniards and of Indigenous allies such as the Tlaxcaltecs, and he thought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-de-sahagun">Bernardino de Sahagún</a> too sympathetic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>.</p><p>The book describes the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, the Aztec palaces, marketplaces and gardens, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> and cannibalism he says he witnessed. Of the ruined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> he wrote that nothing remained of the wonders he had seen. It was published in 1632. Written decades afterwards and containing inaccuracies and biases, it is one of only two first-hand accounts of the Spanish overthrow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bernal Díaz del Castillo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Much of this wealth passed through the great market of Tlatelolco, Tenochtitlan's twin city, whose scale and variety astonished the Spanish conquistador <b>Bernal Díaz del Castillo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its market, described by Cort <b>Díaz del Castillo</b>, reportedly drew some 60,000 people a day, trading food, cloth, obsidian, feathers and slaves.</blockquote><a href="/state/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-del-campo">Medina del Campo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigua-guatemala">Antigua Guatemala</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.31,-4.92&amp;year=1551" aria-label="Medina del Campo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bernal Díaz del Castillo"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4685 4032h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4685 4032h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.31,-4.92&amp;year=1551">What was here in 1551? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…all these buildings resembled the fairy castles we read of in Amadis de Gaul; so high, majestic, and splendid did the temples, towers, and houses of the town, all built of massive stone and lime, rise up out of the midst of the lake.&#8221;</p><footer>The True History of the Conquest of New Spain (first sight of Tenochtitlan, 1519) &#183; tr. John Ingram Lockhart, 1844 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32474/pg32474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it is impossible to speak coolly of things which we had never seen nor heard of, nor even could have dreamt of, beforehand.&#8221;</p><footer>The True History of the Conquest of New Spain (first sight of Tenochtitlan, 1519) &#183; tr. John Ingram Lockhart, 1844 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32474/pg32474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If I were desirous of relating every circumstance I should never finish, and my book would resemble 'Amadis de Gaule,' and other such romances, whose authors can find no end to their pretty stories.&#8221;</p><footer>The True History of the Conquest of New Spain (the siege of Tenochtitlan) &#183; tr. John Ingram Lockhart, 1844 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32475/pg32475-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Our astonishment was indeed raised to the highest pitch, and we could not help remarking to each other, that all these buildings resembled the fairy castles we read of in Amadis de Gaul…&#8221;</p><footer>The True History of the Conquest of New Spain, on first seeing Tenochtitlan (1519) &#183; tr. John Ingram Lockhart, 1844 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32474/pg32474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it is better to believe an eyewitness than an author who writes about things he never saw.&#8221;</p><footer>The True History of the Conquest of New Spain, against Gómara's history &#183; tr. John Ingram Lockhart, 1844 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32474/pg32474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Even now, in my old age, the heroic deeds we then accomplished come vividly to my memory. I imagine I see all passing before me now…&#8221;</p><footer>The True History of the Conquest of New Spain &#183; tr. John Ingram Lockhart, 1844 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32474/pg32474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
